,"summary":{"white":"Tunar Davudov","black":"Pawel Sowinski","whiteElo":"2430","blackElo":"2473","result":"1-0","opening":"Caro-Kann Defense: Tartakower Variation","as":"w","accuracy":{"w":88,"b":83.7},"counts":{"w":{"best":23,"great":4,"brilliant":1,"miss":3},"b":{"best":25,"great":1,"blunder":3,"mistake":1}},"moment":{"name":"30.Be5","cls":"brilliant","color":"w","fen":"2r3k1/pp1RPpbp/5qp1/4Bp2/8/P5PP/1P3P2/5RK1 b - - 2 30","from":"f4","to":"e5"},"finalFen":"1R6/p4pk1/6p1/6q1/1P1b1pP1/P6Q/5PK1/8 b - - 4 44"},"white":"Tunar Davudov","black":"Pawel Sowinski","labels":["book","book","book","book","book","book","book","book","book","book","book","book","book","best","best","best","best","best","excellent","best","best","best","best","best","best","good","best","best","best","best","best","best","great","best","best","excellent","great","best","best","best","good","best","best","good","inaccuracy","best","best","excellent","best","great","excellent","blunder","best","best","best","best","great","best","brilliant","best","great","best","best","best","miss","mistake","miss","blunder","miss","blunder","best","best","excellent","best","good","best","best","inaccuracy","best","good","best","excellent","best","best","best","best","best"],"moments":[{"ply":33,"name":"17.Bxe4","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"17.Bxe4! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Takes the knight on e4.","Protects the queen on g3, which was hanging.","The next best move, 17.Qf3, would drop White's winning chances from 56% to 43% (−0.79).","17.Bxe4 stops …Nxg3.","Black was threatening …Nxg3, winning the queen for a piece and a pawn. Bxe4 takes care of it.","Trades bishop for knight."]},{"ply":37,"name":"19.Qf3","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"19.Qf3! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Moves the attacked queen to safety.","The next best move, 19.Qh3, would drop White's winning chances from 58% to 23% (−3.21).","19.Qf3 gets the queen out of the way of …Rxg3.","Black was threatening …Rxg3, winning the queen for 6 points of material. The queen steps away.","Black answers 19…Bd7.","Engine line: 19…Bd7 20.h3 Rc4 21.Rfe1 Qh4 22.Qe2 (+0.78)."]},{"ply":50,"name":"25…Qxg3","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"25…Qxg3! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Takes the queen on g3.","The next best move, 25…Qa2, would drop Black's winning chances from 50% to 26% (+2.82).","25…Qxg3 trades off the attacked queen instead of losing it to Qxb3.","White was threatening Qxb3, winning the queen. The queen takes something with it instead.","Trades queens.","The queen on g3 can be taken by the f2 pawn or the bishop on f4."]},{"ply":52,"name":"26…Rxe7","cls":"blunder","color":"b","headline":"26…Rxe7 is a blunder: Black's winning chances go from 51% to 26%.","points":["White answers 27.dxe7, which takes the rook on e7.","After dxe7, White wins 8 points of material within 4 moves.","Better was 26…Kf8 (−0.08): the king steps out of check to f8."],"better":"26…Kf8 prepares …f6 and …Kf7 and steps the king out of check to f8. After 27.fxg3 f6 28.Rb1 Kf7 29.b3 Rc3, Black is a queen's worth down (−0.08)."},{"ply":57,"name":"29.g3","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"29.g3! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Attacks the queen.","Protects the bishop on f4, which was hanging.","The next best move, 29.Rfd1, would drop White's winning chances from 79% to 54% (+0.49).","29.g3 threatens gxh4, winning the queen, and stops …Bf6.","It threatens gxh4, winning the queen, so Black has to spend the next move on it.","Black was threatening …Bf6, winning a pawn, picking it up with 30…Qxh3. g3 takes care of it."]},{"ply":59,"name":"30.Be5","cls":"brilliant","color":"w","headline":"30.Be5!! A real sacrifice: White gives up material and the engine still rates it best.","points":["Puts the bishop on e5 where it can be taken.","Black can take, but after 30…Qxe5 31.Rd8+ Rxd8 32.exd8=Q+ the engine rates it +3.75.","Protects the b2 pawn, which was hanging.","30.Be5 threatens Bxf6, winning the queen for a bishop.","It threatens Bxf6, winning the queen for a bishop, so Black has to spend the next move on it.","The bishop on e5 can be taken by the queen on f6."],"why":[{"title":"The idea","text":"30.Be5!! offers the bishop on e5. Taking it lets White's pawn become a queen."},{"title":"What White gives up","text":"30.Be5 puts the bishop on e5 where it can be taken by the queen on f6. That's a bishop on offer."},{"title":"What it does instead","text":"It threatens Bxf6, winning the queen for a bishop."},{"title":"If Black takes the bishop","text":"30…Qxe5 is met by 31.Rd8+. Then 32.exd8=Q+ makes a new queen with check. White ends up a rook's worth of material ahead: White is clearly better (+2.77). This is Black's best try."},{"title":"Without the sacrifice","text":"The best move without the sacrifice, 30.Rfd1, is clearly weaker (+2.78 against +3.75). Black answers 30…Re8, taking the half-open e-file, aiming at the e7 pawn. White would still be clearly better, but far less than after 30.Be5."}]},{"ply":61,"name":"31.Rd8+","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"31.Rd8+! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Check, and Black has only two legal replies.","The next best move, 31.Rfd1, would drop White's winning chances from 77% to 6% (−7.45).","31.Rd8+ gives check, leaving two replies.","Black answers 31…Rxd8, taking the rook on d8.","After 31…Rxd8 32.exd8=Q+ Bf8 33.Qd2 Bc5 34.Kh2, the rooks are traded and White comes out 8 points of material up (+3.17)."]},{"ply":70,"name":"35…h5","cls":"blunder","color":"b","headline":"35…h5 is a blunder: Black's winning chances go from 39% to 15%.","points":["White answers 36.Rd1: the rook takes the open d-file, aiming at the bishop on d6 behind the queen on d3.","Moves a pawn from in front of the king, loosening its cover.","Better was 35…fxg3 (+1.27), which takes the pawn on g3."],"better":"35…fxg3 trades pawns and pushes the pawn storm towards the king. Engine line: 36.Qxg3 Qd5+ 37.Qf3 Qg5+ 38.Qg4 Qd5+ (+1.27)."}],"opening":{"name":"Caro-Kann Defense: Tartakower Variation","eco":"B15","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 7.a3: 13 moves in all, 7 by White and 6 by Black.","Black left the book with 7…g6. In 53 games from this position, strong players played 7…Nc7 (47%), 7…Bd6 (45%) or 7…Bf5 (6%).","The main line goes on 7…Nc7 8.c4.","From here White scored 42% in those games, with 27% drawn."]}}